Z||000007||Lou Christopher||09-28-2001||08:46 AM||||    The radiator was 270 + 15 shipping.  It is really a Howe radiator but I bought it from the man who makes them for Howe.  It is a bit custom in that it has very small tanks on the sides to maximize core area.  It has the 1 1/4 inlet outlet.  Sorry I did not weigh it but I will call the mfg and get a weight.  This is as big a core as you can fit in a Fiero without major mods.  thanks to all for the good thoughts.<BR>                Lou<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||09-29-2001||12:22 AM||||Looks great, hope the maker will consider making it a 'stocked' part, or at least give your design a part number.  <P>"Yeah, give me the one Lou had you make!!"<P>How's the fit, straight bolt-in and go?<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||rockcrawl||09-29-2001||08:24 AM||||I doubt that it weighs any less than the stock plastic/aluminum radiator. In fact I'd bet it weighs more. I have a Griffin aluminum radiator that is smaller than the Fiero radiator and it's several pounds heavier. Don't forget that the core width has been doubled, plus the added coolant capacity increases weight. I missed the first half of the story, but I'm sure Lou didn't do it for weight savings.||||reg||1
